platform/x86: ideapad-laptop: stop erroring on Fast + Long_Life together

ideapad_psy_ext_get_prop() treats the EC reporting both Rapid Charge and
Conservation Mode simultaneously as an invalid state (dev_err + -EINVAL on
every read of charge_types that catches it). On this hardware that
combination is real and intentional, not a fault: Conservation caps the
charge ceiling (e.g. 80%), while Rapid Charge governs how fast the battery
gets there below that cap. The EC toggles Rapid on and off on its own as
part of that behaviour, so the -EINVAL fired repeatedly (confirmed: every
~5s) on a perfectly normal, user-configured charging setup, and any
userspace reading charge_types during that window got a bare I/O error
instead of a sensible value.

Fix: drop the error path and let the existing priority-ordered checks below
it run. Fast is reported when both are active, since it is the operative
charging behaviour in that state; Long_Life is the governing limit, not
itself a description of how the battery is currently charging.

Confirmed on Google/LOQ 15IRH8: charge_types now reads cleanly as
"Fast Standard [Long_Life]" instead of returning -EINVAL, and the repeating
dev_err is gone, with charging behaviour completely unchanged.

Signed-off-by: BobZKernel <bobgenaw420@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/platform/x86/lenovo/ideapad-laptop.c b/drivers/platform/x86/lenovo/ideapad-laptop.c
index 4fbc904f1..52b8fa58a 100644
--- a/drivers/platform/x86/lenovo/ideapad-laptop.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/x86/lenovo/ideapad-laptop.c
@@ -2095,12 +2095,16 @@ static int ideapad_psy_ext_get_prop(struct power_supply *psy,
 			   test_bit(GBMD_RAPID_CHARGE_STATE_BIT, &result));
 	is_conservation = test_bit(GBMD_CONSERVATION_STATE_BIT, &result);
 
-	if (unlikely(is_rapid_charge && is_conservation)) {
-		dev_err(&priv->platform_device->dev,
-			"unexpected charge_types: both [Fast] and [Long_Life] are enabled\n");
-		return -EINVAL;
-	}
-
+	/*
+	 * Fast + Long_Life together is a real, intentional EC state on this
+	 * hardware, not a fault: Conservation caps the charge ceiling (e.g.
+	 * 80%) while Rapid Charge governs how quickly it gets there below
+	 * that cap. The EC toggles Rapid on and off on its own as part of
+	 * that behaviour, so treating the combination as -EINVAL logged a
+	 * dev_err on every poll that caught it mid-toggle. Report Fast: it
+	 * is the active charging behaviour; Long_Life is the governing limit,
+	 * not itself a "the battery is charging like this" state.
+	 */
 	if (is_rapid_charge)
 		val->intval = POWER_SUPPLY_CHARGE_TYPE_FAST;
 	else if (is_conservation)
